# CSI St. Mark's Portal — AWS EC2 demo configuration.
#
#   cp .env.aws.example .env.aws        then edit .env.aws
#
# Then pass it to every Compose command:
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env.aws -f docker-compose.aws.yml up -d
#
# The flag is required. Compose reads `.env` for variable substitution and
# nothing else, so without it this file is ignored in silence and the compose
# file's built-in defaults apply instead. Those defaults are working ones — the
# Portal starts correctly with no environment file at all — so the flag is what
# makes YOUR overrides take effect, not what makes it start.
#
# `.env.aws` is git-ignored. Put no real secrets in THIS file; it is committed.
#
# Every value here is optional. The container generates what it needs on first
# boot and keeps it in the `portal-data` volume, so an unconfigured demo is
# still a working and non-insecure one. The two worth setting are marked.


# ------------------------------------------------------------ worth setting --

# WORTH SETTING. The origin browsers use to reach the Portal — scheme and host,
# no trailing slash, no path. Include the port, because visitors do.
#
# It is stored inside media URLs in the database. Leaving it empty is safe: the
# entrypoint detects the address and fills it in before the API starts, and
# media URLs are rewritten to the requesting origin on every response either
# way. Setting it just makes the values written into the database the right ones.
#
# Replace with the instance's own public IPv4 address:
#     PUBLIC_URL=http://<EC2_PUBLIC_IP>:8080
#
# Not sure yet? Start once, note the address, set it, restart — stored image
# URLs are repointed automatically on the next boot.
PUBLIC_URL=

# WORTH SETTING, eventually. Browser origins allowed to call the API:
# comma-separated, exact match, so include the `www.` form if you serve one.
#
# Left empty, the compose file's `*` default applies — any site may read the
# API. That is what a demo reached by bare IP needs, and it is why this is not
# marked required. Narrow it to real origins once there is a domain.
CORS_ORIGINS=


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ optional --

# Session signing keys. Leave EMPTY and the container generates two different
# 48-byte values on first boot and stores them in the portal-data volume, which
# is the right answer for a demo. Set them only to keep sessions alive across a
# volume reset.
#
# Two DIFFERENT values, 32+ characters each. The API refuses to start if they
# match each other, or if either is one of the placeholders published in this
# repository:
#     openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET=
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=

# The administrator account, created ONCE and only if the database has no
# administrator at all. The snapshot baked into the image already contains the
# church's accounts, so on a normal boot the password is generated, unused and
# thrown away. Set both to have a known login on a freshly reset volume.
#
# Minimum 12 characters, and change it in the CMS afterwards — editing it here
# later has no effect, because by then the account exists.
#
# Leave the email as it is or set a real one, but do not blank it: the compose
# file supplies this same default because the API reads it with `??`, which
# falls back on undefined but not on an empty string.
SEED_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@csistmarksmadipakkam.org
SEED_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# MongoDB's WiredTiger cache, in GB. 0.25 is the floor MongoDB accepts and what
# the entrypoint pins by default — right for a few hundred documents on a small
# instance, and deliberately low because WiredTiger otherwise sizes its cache
# from host RAM and is OOM-killed. Raise it only if the dataset grows a lot.
MONGO_CACHE_GB=0.25


# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set these on the command line, not here
#
#   PORTAL_IMAGE
#       The image tag to run. Compose substitutes `image:` before it consults
#       any env file, so a value here would not be seen. To skip the build and
#       run the published image instead — useful on a small instance, where
#       compiling takes 10-20 minutes and ~2 GB of RAM:
#
#         PORTAL_IMAGE=stmarksdev/csistmarkscmsportal:1.5 \
#           docker compose -f docker-compose.aws.yml up -d --no-build
#
#       That image is private: `docker login -u stmarksdev` first. Leave it
#       unset to use the local `csistmarkscmsportal:1.5` the build produces.
#
#
# Deliberately not variables here
#
#   MONGODB_URI   An external database instead of the one inside the image.
#                 It is commented out in docker-compose.aws.yml rather than
#                 defaulted to empty, because the API reads it with `??` and an
#                 empty string is not undefined — MONGODB_URI="" would hand
#                 mongoose an empty connection string and kill the container on
#                 boot. Uncomment the line in the compose file and give it a
#                 real value; the bundled MongoDB is then never started.
#   PORT          Fixed at 8080 in docker-compose.aws.yml, on both sides of the
#                 mapping. Changing it means changing `ports` to match.
#   SPLIT_PORTS   Would publish the API and CMS separately on 4000 and 3001.
#                 This deployment serves both behind the single 8080 port, so
#                 there is one origin and no CORS between the halves.
#   ENABLE_API_DOCS   Publishes Swagger at /docs, which maps every admin route
#                 for anyone who looks. Off in production by default; set it to
#                 `true` only if the demo is meant to show the API.
#   CORS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_NETWORK   The API refuses to start in production with
#                 this on, because it admits any private-network origin with
#                 credentials. An empty value counts as ON — another reason it
#                 is not listed as a blank variable here.
#   WEBSITE_REVALIDATE_URL / WEBSITE_REVALIDATE_SECRET
#                 Tell the public Next.js website to drop its caches after a
#                 publish. That website is not part of this deployment, so
#                 there is nothing to notify.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
